**Indexed geometry and why indices exist**

Consider two triangles making a quad:

$$
v_0=(-1,-1),\;
v_1=(1,-1),\;
v_2=(1,1),\;
v_3=(-1,1).
$$

Without indices:

```text
Triangle 0: v0 v1 v2
Triangle 1: v0 v2 v3
```

Six complete vertex records must be referenced.

With indexing:

```cpp
vertices = { v0, v1, v2, v3 };

indices = {
    0, 1, 2,
    0, 2, 3
};
```

Now four vertex records plus six small integer references describe the mesh.

$$
d_N=z_c,
$$

$$
d_F=w_c-z_c.
$$

Then a point is inside a particular plane when its corresponding $d$ is non-negative.

This unifies clipping into one operation.

---

**Clipping an edge against a plane**

Suppose edge endpoints are $A$ and $B$.

Their signed plane values are

$$
d_A=d(A),\qquad
d_B=d(B).
$$

The segment is

$$
P(t)=A+t(B-A),\quad t\in[0,1].
$$

Because a clip-plane function is linear,

$$
d(P(t))
=
d_A+t(d_B-d_A).
$$

At the intersection,

$$
d(P(t))=0.
$$

Therefore

$$
d_A+t(d_B-d_A)=0
$$

and

$$
\boxed{
t=
\frac{d_A}{d_A-d_B}
}.
$$

Then

$$
\boxed{
P=A+t(B-A)
}.
$$

That formula is the entire mathematical core of polygon clipping against one linear half-space.

---

**Canonical clipping code**

```cpp
struct ClipVertex
{
    Vec4 position;     // x_c, y_c, z_c, w_c

    // These are outputs produced by the pre-rasterization stage.
    // When clipping introduces a new vertex, they must be interpolated
    // consistently along the original primitive edge.
    Vec3 worldPosition;
    Vec3 normal;
    Vec2 uv;
};

template<class PlaneDistance>
std::vector<ClipVertex> ClipAgainstPlane(
    std::span<const ClipVertex> input,
    PlaneDistance distance)
{
    std::vector<ClipVertex> output;

    if (input.empty())
        return output;

    ClipVertex previous = input.back();
    float dPrevious = distance(previous.position);
    bool previousInside = dPrevious >= 0.0f;

    for (const ClipVertex& current : input)
    {
        const float dCurrent = distance(current.position);
        const bool currentInside = dCurrent >= 0.0f;

        // Crossing the plane means one endpoint is inside and the
        // other is outside. Generate the intersection vertex.
        if (currentInside != previousInside)
        {
            const float denominator = dPrevious - dCurrent;

            // dPrevious / (dPrevious - dCurrent)
            // is obtained by solving d(A + t(B-A)) = 0.
            const float t = dPrevious / denominator;

            ClipVertex intersection;

            intersection.position =
                previous.position +
                t * (current.position - previous.position);

            intersection.worldPosition =
                previous.worldPosition +
                t * (current.worldPosition - previous.worldPosition);

            intersection.normal =
                previous.normal +
                t * (current.normal - previous.normal);

            intersection.uv =
                previous.uv +
                t * (current.uv - previous.uv);

            output.push_back(intersection);
        }

        if (currentInside)
            output.push_back(current);

        previous       = current;
        dPrevious      = dCurrent;
        previousInside = currentInside;
    }

    return output;
}
```

This is essentially Sutherland–Hodgman clipping applied in homogeneous space.

For a complete Vulkan-style view volume, apply six planes sequentially.

**Fragment shader invocation**

Once a covered location survives enough rasterization setup, the implementation can generate a fragment shader invocation.

Conceptually the shader receives interpolated inputs:

```glsl
layout(location=0) in vec3 inColor;
layout(location=1) in vec3 inNormal;
layout(location=2) in vec3 inWorldPosition;
layout(location=3) in vec4 inLightClipPosition;
layout(location=4) in vec2 inTexCoord;
```

That is exactly the current `triangle.frag` interface in your Kairo renderer.

**Fragment shader invocation**

Once a covered location survives enough rasterization setup, the implementation can generate a fragment shader invocation.

Conceptually the shader receives interpolated inputs:

```glsl
layout(location=0) in vec3 inColor;
layout(location=1) in vec3 inNormal;
layout(location=2) in vec3 inWorldPosition;
layout(location=3) in vec4 inLightClipPosition;
layout(location=4) in vec2 inTexCoord;
```

That is exactly the current `triangle.frag` interface in your Kairo renderer.

The fragment shader does **not** receive the three original vertices.

The rasterizer has already transformed those per-vertex outputs into per-fragment interpolated values.

That distinction separates the pre-rasterization and post-rasterization stages.

---

**Why draw-call overhead exists**

Suppose you have 50,000 identical rocks.

A naive renderer performs:

```cpp
for (const Rock& rock : rocks)
{
    BindPipeline();
    BindMesh();
    BindMaterial();
    SetTransform(rock.transform);
    DrawIndexed();
}
```

Even if each rock contains little geometry, the CPU must repeatedly:

```text
select state
encode commands
validate objects
write/update descriptors or constants
call API
manage command-buffer state
```

A draw call is not fundamentally expensive because “function calls are slow.”

It is expensive because it represents **submission and state-management work**.

If GPU geometry work becomes tiny but CPU submission remains one command per object, the renderer becomes CPU-bound.

---

**Indirect drawing**

In ordinary drawing the CPU records parameters directly:

```cpp
vkCmdDrawIndexed(
    cmd,
    indexCount,
    instanceCount,
    firstIndex,
    vertexOffset,
    firstInstance);
```

Indirect drawing moves those parameters into GPU-visible memory.

For indexed drawing, the conceptual command record is:

```cpp
struct DrawIndexedIndirectCommand
{
    uint32_t indexCount;
    uint32_t instanceCount;
    uint32_t firstIndex;
    int32_t  vertexOffset;
    uint32_t firstInstance;
};
```

Then:

```cpp
vkCmdDrawIndexedIndirect(..., indirectBuffer, ...);
```

causes the device to read drawing parameters from the buffer.

Vulkan defines indirect commands precisely this way: draw parameters are fetched by the device from a buffer during execution rather than embedded in the command recording itself. [docs.vulkan.org](https://docs.vulkan.org/refpages/latest/refpages/source/vkCmdDrawIndirect.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Why is that transformative?

**Compute-driven culling example**

Suppose we have one million instances.

Each instance stores:

```cpp
struct Instance
{
    BoundingSphere bounds;
    Mat4 transform;
    uint32_t meshID;
};
```

A compute invocation handles one instance.

Conceptually:

```glsl
uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;

if (i >= instanceCount)
    return;

Instance instance = instances[i];

if (!IntersectsFrustum(instance.bounds))
    return;

uint slot = atomicAdd(visibleCount, 1);

visibleInstances[slot] = i;
```

Now the compute pass has compacted the visible set.

A later stage constructs indirect draw commands.

The architecture has changed from:

$$
CPU\rightarrow\text{one command per object}
$$

to:

$$
CPU\rightarrow\text{dispatch}
\rightarrow
GPU\text{ visibility}
\rightarrow
GPU\text{ command data}
\rightarrow
GPU\text{ draws}.
$$

---

**Storage buffers**

Storage buffers are more general.

They can hold large arrays and can support reads and writes depending on shader/API configuration.

For GPU-driven rendering:

```glsl
layout(std430, binding = 4) readonly buffer Instances
{
    InstanceData instances[];
};
```

could store hundreds of thousands of instances.

A compute shader could write:

```glsl
layout(std430, binding = 5) buffer VisibleList
{
    uint visible[];
};
```

The philosophical difference is useful:

$$
\text{uniform buffer}
\approx
\text{small structured shared constants}
$$

whereas

$$
\text{storage buffer}
\approx
\text{general shader-visible structured memory}.
$$

Modern APIs blur the exact performance distinction, but the usage model remains valuable.

Your current main Kairo mesh path does not yet depend on storage-buffer-driven instance/draw generation.

